#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087578 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087851 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d9d8d4d2-fef7-5ada-8691-3f504cb3cf97" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Horizontal Asymptote When the Degrees of the Numerator and Denominator are Equal"}] :question/id #uuid "eb0b899e-af3a-587c-96ae-f1da9e0929dd" :question/math-academy-id "e-5847" :question/problem "Determine the horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x) = \\dfrac{4x^3-x}{3x^3+24}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to\\infty.$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is $x^3.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by $x^3,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{4{x}^{3}-x}{3{x}^{3}+24} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{x}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{3{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{24}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(4-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}{(3+\\frac{24}{{x}^{3}})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms $\\dfrac{1}{x^2}$ and $\\dfrac{24}{x^3}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to\\infty,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(4-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}{(3+\\frac{24}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & \\to \\frac{4-0}{3+0} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the horizontal asymptote is $y = \\dfrac{4}{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087579 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087852 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6f75252e-bf7d-59ed-bb16-6a2b65a97863" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Horizontal Asymptote When the Degree of the Denominator is Greater"}] :question/id #uuid "0d53819b-ca24-549d-98e2-a9b78b49c080" :question/math-academy-id "e-5849" :question/problem "Determine the horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x) = \\dfrac{3x^2+ 2x}{6x^4-1}$." :question/worked-solution "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to\\infty.$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is $x^4.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by $x^4,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{3{x}^{2}+2x}{6{x}^{4}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{{x}^{4}}+\\frac{2x}{{x}^{4}})}{(\\frac{6{x}^{4}}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}})}{(6-\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms  $\\dfrac{3}{x^2}, \\dfrac{2}{x^3},$ and $\\dfrac{1}{x^4}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to\\infty,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}})}{(6-\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}})} \\\\  & \\to \\frac{0+0}{6-0} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the horizontal asymptote is $y = 0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087580 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087853 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "653503b2-4631-561e-af5e-847bcbdfe8e2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Horizontal Asymptote When the Degree of the Numerator is Greater"}] :question/id #uuid "ea2a97a7-fce8-5b4a-a269-57e0ae7984d8" :question/math-academy-id "e-5851" :question/problem "Determine the horizontal asymptote of $f(x) = \\dfrac{2x^3}{x^2-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to\\infty.$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is $x^2.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by $x^2,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{{x}^{2}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{(1-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the term $\\dfrac{1}{x^2}$ becomes very small. Therefore, as $x\\to\\infty,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{2x}{(1-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{2x}{1-0} \\\\  & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is not a constant, which means that $y = f(x)$ does not have a horizontal asymptote."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434787 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087853 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "653503b2-4631-561e-af5e-847bcbdfe8e2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Horizontal Asymptote When the Degree of the Numerator is Greater"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2fea007a-0dd4-5f6c-9257-ed12e9d45202" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\,\\frac{1}{17}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434773} {:answer/id #uuid "3576ce64-1716-532c-9b43-4e79dd447ff6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\,\\frac{7}{17}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434774} {:answer/id #uuid "50d16fda-27d0-5acd-9321-077265d3dfcb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No horizontal asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434775} {:answer/id #uuid "81a8f7bc-4cfa-55bc-a03d-5635c11ef30b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434777} {:answer/id #uuid "bf44ca15-0e20-5367-bb70-8f8828b77a1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{10}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434781}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "50d16fda-27d0-5acd-9321-077265d3dfcb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No horizontal asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434775} :answer-field/id #uuid "01e0e053-f6d5-5da3-8737-b2a31b0bda6a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434782}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "240f8824-faa1-5120-9751-29976f966dc7" :question/math-academy-id "q-6994" :question/problem "Determine the horizontal asymptote of $f(x)=\\frac{(2-x)(x+5)}{17x+7}.$" :question/worked-solution "The given function can be rewritten as follows:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{10-3x-{x}^{2}}{17x+7}\n$$\n\nTo find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is $x.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{10-3x-{x}^{2}}{17x+7} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{10}{x}-\\frac{3x}{x}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{x})}{(\\frac{17x}{x}+\\frac{7}{x})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{10}{x}-3-x)}{(17+\\frac{7}{x})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms $\\frac{10}{x}$ and $\\frac{7}{x}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(\\frac{10}{x}-3-x)}{(17+\\frac{7}{x})} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{0-3-x}{17+0} \\\\  & =-\\frac{x+3}{17}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is not a constant, which means that $y=f(x)$ does not have a horizontal asymptote."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088279 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087852 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6f75252e-bf7d-59ed-bb16-6a2b65a97863" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Horizontal Asymptote When the Degree of the Denominator is Greater"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e3a733ff-d529-5f11-83d5-26f3283916e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086329} {:answer/id #uuid "efdb67da-e129-5640-9779-c117b945dab3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = \\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086330} {:answer/id #uuid "0b3a2b1a-ef2a-573d-b248-54b8b0775046" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No horizontal asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086331} {:answer/id #uuid "3e10cd66-0241-5a85-8954-e10915c1bf02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = 7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086332} {:answer/id #uuid "6907de6e-2574-5efe-8637-7c4f30a018a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = \\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086333}] :answer-field/id #uuid "8243a955-7658-5fc2-b22c-f540ebc504d9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086328}] :question/id #uuid "cd34e155-3ce2-5319-8a89-39c8d0e8465a" :question/math-academy-id "q-6999" :question/problem "Determine the horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x) = (7x^{2} + 4)/(x(3x^{2} + 12))$."}]}
